<p>Early Mandan leaders understood the economic stability that a federal facility could bring to the area. In 1908, they had a bill introduced in Congress to establish an Indian school. The legislation passed, but Bismarck business leaders maneuvered to have the school built on their side of the Missouri River, which had no bridge connecting the two cities at the time. Mandan's promoters did not welcome the outcome.</p>

<p>Business leaders also understood that their own success depended on the success of farmers and ranchers. Early Dakota settlers brought favorite varieties of fruits, vegetables, grains, and trees from their former homes, but many did not thrive under conditions on the high prairie. Efforts to secure a federal facility therefore turned toward regional agriculture.</p>

<p>{% person "andrew-e-thorberg" %}A. E. Thorberg{% endperson %}, {% person "william-a-lanterman" %}W. A. Lanterman{% endperson %}, and {% person "charles-lewis-c-l-timmerman" %}C. L. Timmerman{% endperson %} pressed for an agricultural experiment station after a prairie fire devastated many acres. They lobbied Congress the following year for a research laboratory that would develop improved plant strains. The bill failed, as it did again in 1911. A bill establishing the Northern Great Plains Research Laboratory finally passed in August 1912, although funding the facility required a separate effort.</p>

<p>Mandan businesses pooled their resources and purchased land for approximately $32 per acre. The contributors included a bank, several mercantile companies, a drugstore, an insurance company, a newspaper, a clothing store, and a tailor. {% person "james-martin-hanley-sr" %}James Hanley{% endperson %}, a prominent area politician, persuaded the state to reimburse them. The land became state property and was leased to the federal government for 99 years.</p>

