<p>The first buildings were erected in spring 1913, when workers also planted a windbreak a quarter-mile long and 14 rows wide. Scientists broke sod that year and planted several blocks of trees in 1914. Although the facility had been established, Congress had appropriated no money for research. The first agronomist, {% person "j-c-brinsmeade" %}J. C. Brinsmeade{% endperson %}, initially purchased equipment and hired workers with his own money.</p>

<p>{% person "robert-wilson" %}Robert Wilson{% endperson %} placed newspaper advertisements offering landowners saplings and planting plans through the Windbreak Tree Program. More than 4,000 landowners applied. Wilson and five forestry students inspected each site, traveling by horse and buggy, train, and automobile. The following spring, more than 1,300 farms in North Dakota, South Dakota, Wyoming, and Montana planted windbreaks.</p>

<p>In later years, fruit breeding produced hardier apples, plums, and apricots. Improved vegetables helped support local seed companies such as Oscar H. Will and Company, while research on grains and other crops produced greater resistance to drought and disease.</p>

