    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/northern-great-plains-experiment-station/200-gpaes-bill-approved.jpg" alt="Document concerning approval of the experiment station">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Mandan owed much to Thorberg's promotion of the station and to Representative {% person "louis-benjamin-hanna" %}L. B. Hanna{% endperson %} and Senators {% person "asle-gronna" %}Asle Gronna{% endperson %} and {% person "porter-j-mccumber" %}Porter J. McCumber{% endperson %}, who helped obtain the necessary congressional appropriation. The Northern Great Plains Experiment Station became the nation's largest federal dryland experiment station outside Beltsville, Maryland.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/northern-great-plains-experiment-station/250-gpaes-africian-violet.jpg" alt="Flower variety associated with the experiment station">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The station conducted research into all phases of dryland farming and developed new grains, fruits, and vegetables. Commercially released fruits included five apple varieties, three crabapples, four plums, two cherry plums, an apricot, and several currants. Although its principal focus remained food crops, the station also released hardy flower varieties.</p>

<p>Bismarck-based Oscar H. Will and Company benefited from its proximity to the experiment station. An article about the seed company's history is available from <a href="https://www.farmcollector.com/farm-life/Seed-Corn-to-Shelterbelts.aspx">Farm Collector</a>.</p>

<p>The station has played an important role in advancing food production. The Northern Great Plains Research Laboratory later assumed an additional role in green-energy research, including carbon sequestration and switchgrass varieties for potential ethanol feedstocks. Other projects included improving the nutritional value of beef, reducing production steps while increasing crop yields, and creating new forage cultivars.</p>
